// ===========================================================================
// SECTION 1 of languages/lib/kotlin-rt.metajs -- NUMBERS AND OPERATORS
//
// The authoritative specification is abnf/jsrtkotlin.go (the function each one
// ports is named at its head) plus the two neutral externs Kotlin's emitter
// reaches from abnf/jsrt.go (js_jadd, js_jband). Every helper here is prefixed
// k1; the shared names (ktNorm, ktWU, ktNum, ktIsInt, ktRaise, ktStructEq, ...)
// are section 0's and only CALLED here. ktRender is section 2's; ktSeqMethod /
// ktSetMethod / ktMapMethod are section 3's. This section OWNS ktNumMethod.
//
// Kotlin's Long is SIGNED, so no jl*-style workaround layer is owed: plain sintOp /
// sintCmp / sintStr / sintNum / sintConv give the right answers on a signed 64 bit
// box, and this section calls them directly. Every result goes through section
// 0's ktNorm, which is where the boxed-Long decision lives (see there). The two
// places that need an exact 64 bit CONSTANT (Long.MIN_VALUE / MAX_VALUE, for the
// saturating float narrowing) build it with the tag's own shift.
//
// Not here: ktTypeObj (the companion-constant table, a GLOBALS concern) and
// ktSumOf (section 3's).
// ===========================================================================

// ----- operand readers ------------------------------------------------------

// jsrtint.go giVal (105) at the value level: a Char is its code point, every
// other numeric passes through and the floor's own si_val reads it. The {__char}
// box is the ONE thing no sint*/flo* builtin knows.
function k1Val(v) {
    if (ktIsChar(v)) { return ktCharCode(v) }
    return v
}

